welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and today our  guest is Professor Ali Sowinski a  professor of economics and the  co-director of the macroeconomic  research program at the Cowell's  foundation at yale university professor  Sowinski is here to talk with us about  his new research on Russian values and  their attitudes toward the west welcome  professor Sowinski well it's better to  be here so in a nutshell your research  has shown Russians have negative values  towards the west give us the highlights  of your findings so our research shows  that Russians do not like the West they  do not like the democracy they do not  like the free markets they do not like  the Western economic and social model  for their society at the same time which  is paradoxically the very very poor  capitalist in the real life they're uber  capitalists in the way they conduct  their daily business and the way they  behave so that's one big fine novella  research the second big find is that the  attitudes of Russians the values of  Russians changed over time so they  became much more against the West much  more against the Western economic and  social model in their attitudes and  there are very interesting vacations for  wide wireless cell so this differs from  20 years ago when Russians were pretty  pro West why the change actually the 20  year ago the first research on the  values of rush the first serious  research in the valence of Russian was  conducted by my colleague Bob Shiller  here at Yale his co-authors from Russia  so they have conducted a series of four  interviews where they talked to people  in Moscow and thurs work and some  Ukrainian cities and they asked about  their values towards the west and just  in general about their valises the first  serious research on the values of  reference so they showed that there is  no such thing as homeless of Atticus  there is no such thing as Soviet man and  in the beginning of the 90s the edge of  the Russians was very much  for was very much similar to the ones  that person Europe or a person in the US  would have now the situation has  drastically changed we see that in the  surveys conducted in 2008 Russians are  very negative towards the west and  moreover this attitude becomes worse and  worse and worse even compared to say  2004 so we have seen a drastic reversals  of the values of the attitudes that  Russians have let's talk about your  methodology how did you gather the data  so they did we have is actually are  quite interesting because I pull up set  posing to the different from the usual  surveys at having a one or two thousand  members who are asked about their values  who are asked about their attitudes have  a very very large data set we have about  forty thirty four thousand people who  are asked about all these questions and  the survey is conducted by very large  Russian polling organization that does  political polling it's called the public  opinion foundation so it's 64 regions  rural urban people of all social  backgrounds etc etc so it's very very  detailed data not only asking about the  values but also about all kinds of  social and economic characteristics of  people so in some sense we can zoom in  into the mind of an average Russian and  see what they're thinking about so  moreover this this foundation is very  motivated very well funded very  motivated it does political politics one  of the biggest political pollsters in  Russia so we have spectacular data set  and moreover the data set that spans  from two thousand three to two thousand  eight so for example compared to the  telephone surveys we have much more  detailed and much more comprehensive  data what did you find most surprising  when you analyze the data so one thing  they actually shocked me was that the  attitudes the negative attitudes towards  the west are the highest among the  oldest generation and also the youngest  generation and people who are the most  positive towards the west are the people  between age 35 and 45 so one could say  well how could this be  the guys were born you know who are 35  to 45 now the boring Soviet Union they  were probably brainwashed by the Soviet  propaganda which portrayed West has been  evil negative militarist etc so why are  there is so much more positive than say  the younger generation who grew up with  McDonald's western movies all kinds of  TV shows MTV and the explanation we have  is as follows so people who are 35 to 45  they have seen the negative part of the  Soviet Soviet Society the absence  complete absence of freedom Russian you  know food very low consumption or low  diversity of consumption so they know  what it is to live in the Soviet Union  the younger generation I romanticize the  Soviet Union so they don't see the  negative parts of it but the only I know  they only see the positive parts of it  so so they're as negative as the very  old generation who lost from the from  the fall of the Soviet Union so it's the  first surprising part the second  surprising part is how drastically the  values of Russian change the negative  attitudes of the Russians changed in  this 20 years we talked a little bit  about that already and the explanation I  think is is very interesting for this so  think about the 90s in the 90s there  were all kinds of liberal economic  reforms democratization the society  moved from a very rigid soviet socialist  society to essentially the free market  to the wild west-style free market at  the same time the people experienced  very difficult economic times the gdp  fell the consumption fell then and 2000  coincided the the oil prices one top and  also here are some other economic  reports by then but at the same time the  democratization the liberal economic  reforms were pulled back so an average  Russian is thinking the fall of an you  know in the 90s the were there was free  market but we live very badly in two  thousand to two thousand eight the  country experienced spectacular gross at  seven percent per year for ten years so  well it must be the case that democracy  and the free markets are bad and the  strong government  the pull back of the liberal reform the  State Corporation etc are good so so we  see this the economy affecting the  beliefs and the values of the Russians  and what is interesting also that Russia  actually not the only country that has a  similar phenomenon so French economist  at goosetown line the air and they with  the smart documented something similar  for France as well so in France in the  post-war period the post-second World  War period the economy grew very well  and it coincided with a larger  involvement of the government in the  economy and you can still see that  French think that say compared to the US  or compared to Britain that if the  government involvement in the economy is  much better so we see that this  correlation between economic growth  feeding into the values of both Russians  and French and for Russians it's much  more drastic so what is important to  look at these values and beliefs so the  key question for Russia is how to  reconcile the dislike for the democracy  and they dislike for economic reforms  that an average Russian has with the the  absolute need to push for such reforms  so Roger is actually pretty rich country  now so in purchasing power parity it's  about thirteen thousand dollars dollars  gdp per capita somewhere between  children or iguana so all the  low-hanging fruits of economic reforms  have been already picked so now to  really grow and this is a very important  question for the society how to grow  fast the the new way new economy has to  be built there has to be more  innovations it has to be more  democratization but on the other hand  the values of Russians pose a threat to  that so how could so if Russia is  democratic than the voters the media and  Russian would say no we don't want the  liberal air force we don't want more  market freedom so in one hand it can be  that either the Russian can be  non-democratic but grow fast according  to the values of Russians or to be  democratic but pull back the economic  reforms so in other words the values of  Russians pose a constraint on economic  growth  just as much as the institutions or  technology fascinating and do you think  policymakers in the United States can  use this research somehow yeah it's very  important to for our for the US  policymakers to understand what's going  with the values of Russians so everybody  thought at least in the beginning of the  ninth is that as Russia will become a  richer more affluent society that the  attitudes towards the west are gonna be  much more positive so in fact Russia has  indeed achieved the level of prosperity  unheard of or unseen in the whole  Russian history so just an average  Russian and listener Russians in big  cities you can travel abroad you know go  to sushi restaurants see western movies  at the same time they have very negative  attitudes towards the west so in fact  much worse attitudes then were at the  beginning of the democratization the  collapse of the Soviet Union so if one  hopes that if Russia becomes even more  prosperous that it will become more  positive towards the west I think it's a  dangerous question so it one has to  think about this as well so the second a  point that comes from our research is  that if one wants to influence how  Russians view the West one has to really  think about what parts of the society  support the Western what what parts  don't so for example you know the the  core constituents is supporting the West  is actually the people 35 to 45 year  olds but the younger generation is lost  in some sense for the positive failures  to the vestment they already are as bad  as the 60 year olds who grow up in the  Soviet Union who lost essentially  everything or many many things with with  the democratization so I think it's very  important to target the younger group  and target their attitudes towards the  west and also to understand that the  core support is actually 35 to 45 year  olds very interesting well thank you  very much for being here today and  sharing your research with us well thank  you for more information about professor  sowinski and his research please visit  our website at  I led you back / Macmillan report be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  you
 

